//? Title: The Bernstein–Vazirani Algorithm
//? Subtitle: Reading a hidden parity in one quantum query, how it degrades under a noisy oracle, and what the same circuit does on a breast-cancer classifier, where it samples the Walsh–Hadamard spectrum, using datamunge's quantum state-vector simulator
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
The Bernstein–Vazirani algorithm reads a hidden \(n\)-bit string \(s\) from an oracle for
\(f(x)=s\cdot x\bmod2\) in one quantum query, where any classical algorithm needs \(n\). We derive
the circuit and show that on any Boolean \(f\) it performs *Fourier sampling*: it returns \(y\) with
probability \(\hat F(y)^2\), the squared Walsh–Hadamard coefficient. On datamunge's state-vector
simulator it recovers \(s\) with probability 1 up to \(n=20\). Under a noisy oracle that is wrong on
a fraction \(\eta\) of inputs, the success probability follows the derived \((1-2\eta)^2\) to four
decimals, and repetition still finds \(s\) at \(\eta=0.4\). Applied to a nearest-neighbour classifier
on ten binarised features of `dm::datasets::breast_cancer()`, 2,000 measurements reproduce its
Fourier spectrum. The circuit's amplitudes match the fast Walsh–Hadamard transform to
\(10^{-15}\), and 57% of the weight sits at degree 0 and 1. A weighted vote of single features
built from the degree-1 coefficients classifies 89.8% of tumours, against 92.6% for the full
classifier.
)

\toc

> The problem

A black box computes a Boolean function \(f:\{0,1\}^n\to\{0,1\}\) promised to be a parity,

$$
f(x)=s\cdot x \bmod 2=s_1x_1\oplus s_2x_2\oplus\dots\oplus s_nx_n ,
$$
\alttext(f of x equals s dot x mod 2, which is s 1 x 1 XOR s 2 x 2 and so on up to s n x n.)

for an unknown string \(s\in\{0,1\}^n\). Classically, the query \(x=e_i\) (a single 1 in position
\(i\)) returns \(s_i\), so \(n\) queries suffice. They are also necessary, even for a randomised
algorithm that may fail with small probability. Each answer is one bit, there are \(2^n\)
equally likely secrets, and after \(k<n\) answers at least \(2^{n-k}\) secrets remain consistent.
Bernstein and Vazirani (1993) showed that a quantum computer needs one query.

> One query

>> The circuit

Index the basis states of \(n\) qubits by \(x\in\{0,1\}^n\). The Hadamard gate sends
\(|0\rangle\mapsto(|0\rangle+|1\rangle)/\sqrt2\) and \(|1\rangle\mapsto(|0\rangle-|1\rangle)/\sqrt2\), so on
each qubit \(H|x_i\rangle=\tfrac1{\sqrt2}\sum_{y_i}(-1)^{x_iy_i}|y_i\rangle\), and on all of them

$$
H^{\otimes n}|x\rangle=\frac1{\sqrt{2^n}}\sum_{y}(-1)^{x\cdot y}|y\rangle .
$$
\alttext(H tensor n applied to the basis state x equals 1 over root 2 to the n, times the sum over y of minus 1 to the x dot y, times the basis state y.)

The oracle is queried as a *phase oracle*, \(U_f|x\rangle=(-1)^{f(x)}|x\rangle\). This is what the
standard bit oracle \(|x\rangle|b\rangle\mapsto|x\rangle|b\oplus f(x)\rangle\) does to an ancilla prepared in
\(|-\rangle=(|0\rangle-|1\rangle)/\sqrt2\): flipping \(|-\rangle\) multiplies it by \(-1\), and the sign
*kicks back* onto \(|x\rangle\). The circuit applies \(H^{\otimes n}\), one oracle call and \(H^{\otimes n}\)
again to \(|0\cdots0\rangle\):

$$
|0\rangle\;\xrightarrow{H^{\otimes n}}\;\frac1{\sqrt{2^n}}\sum_x|x\rangle
\;\xrightarrow{U_f}\;\frac1{\sqrt{2^n}}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}|x\rangle
\;\xrightarrow{H^{\otimes n}}\;\sum_y\Big(\frac1{2^n}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)\oplus x\cdot y}\Big)|y\rangle .
$$
\alttext(The zero state goes under H tensor n to the uniform superposition over x, then under U f to the sum of minus 1 to the f of x times x, then under H tensor n to the sum over y of 1 over 2 to the n times the sum over x of minus 1 to the f of x XOR x dot y, times the state y.)

For a parity, \(f(x)\oplus x\cdot y=(s\oplus y)\cdot x\), and the inner sum is \(2^n\) if \(y=s\) and \(0\)
otherwise, because a non-zero \(z=s\oplus y\) has \(z\cdot x=1\) for exactly half of all \(x\). The
register ends *exactly* in \(|s\rangle\), and one measurement reads all \(n\) bits.

>> Fourier sampling

The final amplitude of \(|y\rangle\) is defined for any \(f\), promise or not:

$$
\hat F(y)=\frac1{2^n}\sum_x(-1)^{f(x)}(-1)^{x\cdot y},\qquad \Pr(\text{measure }y)=\hat F(y)^2,\qquad \sum_y\hat F(y)^2=1 .
$$
\alttext(F hat of y equals 1 over 2 to the n times the sum over x of minus 1 to the f of x times minus 1 to the x dot y. The probability of measuring y is F hat of y squared, and these squares sum to 1.)

\(\hat F\) is the Walsh–Hadamard transform of the \(\pm1\) function \(F=(-1)^f\), the Fourier transform
on \(\mathbb Z_2^n\), and the sum is Parseval's identity. Each coefficient is a correlation:
\(\hat F(y)=1-2\Pr_x[f(x)\ne y\cdot x]\) under uniform \(x\). So the Bernstein–Vazirani circuit
*samples parities in proportion to their squared correlation with \(f\)*. This *Fourier sampling* is
the step that Simon's and Shor's algorithms build on, and the reason quantum examples make some
learning problems easy (Bshouty and Jackson 1999).

A classical computer can compute the whole spectrum with the fast Walsh–Hadamard transform in
\(O(n2^n)\) operations, but that needs all \(2^n\) values of \(f\). `algorithms::walsh_spectrum` does
this. `algorithms::fourier_sampling_state` instead simulates the circuit gate by gate on the
state-vector simulator, and `algorithms::measure_shots` draws measurements from the result.

>> A noisy oracle

Suppose the oracle is wrong on a fixed fraction \(\eta\) of all inputs (the same inputs every time
it is asked). Each wrong input flips one sign in the sum for \(y=s\), so

$$
\hat F(s)=1-2\eta,\qquad \Pr(\text{measure }s)=(1-2\eta)^2 ,
$$
\alttext(F hat of s equals 1 minus 2 eta, and the probability of measuring s is 1 minus 2 eta, squared.)

and the remaining probability \(1-(1-2\eta)^2\) spreads over the other \(2^n-1\) strings. If the
errors are scattered at random, each other \(y\) gets about \(4\eta(1-\eta)/2^n\) on average, and the
largest of them several times that. Repeating the circuit and taking the most frequent outcome
recovers \(s\) whenever \((1-2\eta)^2\) beats the largest competitor, with about
\(1/(1-2\eta)^2\) repetitions needed to see \(s\) at all. The classical analogue, *learning parity with noise*, is believed hard
when samples come at random: the best known algorithms take \(2^{O(n/\log n)}\) time.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bernstein_vazirani_noise.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <chrono>
#include <map>
#include <random>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937_64 rng(1993);

  // 1. Exact recovery and the cost of simulating it.
  std::print("| \\(n\\) | hidden \\(s\\) | quantum result | \\(\\Pr(s)\\) | classical result | classical queries | simulation time |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int n : {4, 8, 12, 16, 20}) {
    const std::size_t s = rng() & ((std::size_t{1} << n) - 1);
    const auto f = alg::parity_truth_table(n, s);
    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    const auto psi = alg::fourier_sampling_state(f);
    const double ms = std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
    int queries = 0;
    const auto classical = alg::bernstein_vazirani_classical(n, [&](std::size_t x) { ++queries; return f[x]; });
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} | {} | {} | {:.1f} ms |\n", n, s, alg::most_probable(psi),
               std::norm(psi[s]), classical, queries, ms);
  }

  // 2. A noisy oracle on n = 10 bits: flip a random fraction eta of the truth table.
  const int n = 10;
  const std::size_t N = std::size_t{1} << n, s = 0b1011001110;
  std::print("\nNoisy oracle, \\(n={}\\), \\(s={}\\). Success of the most frequent outcome over 500 repetitions:\n\n", n, s);
  std::print("| \\(\\eta\\) | \\(\\Pr(s)\\) | \\((1-2\\eta)^2\\) | largest other \\(\\Pr(y)\\) | 1 shot | 3 shots | 10 shots | 30 shots | 100 shots |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> etas, p_s, theory_eta, theory_p;
  for (double eta : {0.0, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, 0.45, 0.5}) {
    auto f = alg::parity_truth_table(n, s);
    std::vector<std::size_t> order(N);
    std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
    std::ranges::shuffle(order, rng);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < static_cast<std::size_t>(std::lround(eta * N)); ++i) f[order[i]] ^= 1;
    const auto psi = alg::fourier_sampling_state(f);
    double other = 0.0;
    for (std::size_t y = 0; y < N; ++y)
      if (y != s) other = std::max(other, std::norm(psi[y]));
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |", eta, std::norm(psi[s]), (1 - 2 * eta) * (1 - 2 * eta), other);
    for (std::size_t shots : {1, 3, 10, 30, 100}) {
      double success = 0;
      for (int trial = 0; trial < 500; ++trial) {
        std::map<std::size_t, int> count;
        for (auto y : alg::measure_shots(psi, shots, rng())) ++count[y];
        // The most frequent outcome, ties broken uniformly at random (credited fractionally).
        int top = 0, tied = 0;
        for (const auto& [y, c] : count) top = std::max(top, c);
        for (const auto& [y, c] : count) tied += c == top;
        if (count.contains(s) && count[s] == top) success += 1.0 / tied;
      }
      std::print(" {:.3f} |", success / 500.0);
    }
    std::print("\n");
    etas.push_back(eta);
    p_s.push_back(std::norm(psi[s]));
  }
  for (int i = 0; i <= 100; ++i) {
    theory_eta.push_back(i / 200.0);
    theory_p.push_back(std::pow(1 - 2 * (i / 200.0), 2));
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .lines(theory_eta, theory_p, "(1 - 2 eta)^2", dm::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 2.0)
      .points(etas, p_s, "simulated circuit", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 8.0)
      .y_limits(0, 1.15)
      .title("Probability of measuring the hidden string")
      .x_label("fraction of oracle answers flipped, eta")
      .y_label("P(measure s)")
      .size(820, 420)
      .save_svg("build/bernstein_vazirani_noise.svg");
}
```
