// result_end

\caption(Probability that the Bernstein–Vazirani circuit returns the hidden string when the oracle is wrong on a fraction \(\eta\) of its inputs: simulated circuit (points) and \((1-2\eta)^2\) (line).)
\alttext(Scatter plot of the probability of measuring the hidden string against the noise fraction eta from 0 to 0.5. The eight simulated points lie on the grey parabola one minus two eta squared, falling from 1 at eta 0 through 0.64 at 0.1 and 0.16 at 0.3 to 0 at 0.5.)

The simulated circuit returns \(s\) with probability 1 to twelve decimals for every \(n\) up to 20,
using one oracle call against the classical algorithm's \(n\). Simulating it is another matter:
the state vector has \(2^n\) amplitudes, and \(n=20\) takes 47 ms on this machine against 2.3 ms at
\(n=16\). That is a factor of 20, which is what \(n2^n\) predicts: \((20/16)\cdot2^4=20\).

With noise, the probability of \(s\) matches \((1-2\eta)^2\) to four decimals. The small differences
come from rounding \(\eta\cdot1024\) flips to a whole number. Repetition works as derived. At
\(\eta=0.1\), ten shots always recover \(s\); at \(\eta=0.3\), 100 shots do; at \(\eta=0.4\), where
\(s\) appears in only 4% of shots, 100 shots succeed 66% of the time. At \(\eta=0.45\) the
largest wrong outcome (0.0142) is *more* likely than \(s\) (0.0099), so no number of repetitions
helps. The threshold is set by the extreme competitor, not the average one. At \(\eta=0.5\) the
oracle is uncorrelated with every parity of \(s\), and \(\hat F(s)=0\) exactly.

> Fourier sampling a breast-cancer classifier

What does the circuit do when \(f\) is not a parity at all? `dm::datasets::breast_cancer()` has
569 tumours, 212 malignant, described by 30 measurements. We take the ten "mean" features,
code each as 1 when it is above its median, and so place every tumour at a corner
\(x\in\{0,1\}^{10}\) of the cube. The oracle \(f(x)\) answers "malignant?" for every corner. Where
tumours fall it uses the majority diagnosis, and empty corners take the majority over the nearest
occupied corners in Hamming distance. This is a nearest-neighbour classifier on the binarised data.
Fourier sampling it shows which *parities of features*, \(\chi_y(x)=(-1)^{y\cdot x}\), the classifier
correlates with. We group the spectrum by degree \(|y|\), the number of features in the parity:

$$
W_k=\sum_{|y|=k}\hat F(y)^2,\qquad \sum_{k=0}^{n}W_k=1,
$$
\alttext(W k is the sum of F hat of y squared over strings y with k ones, and the W k sum to 1.)

and compare the exact spectrum with 2,000 simulated measurements. We also test the
*low-degree* classifiers \(g_d(x)=\operatorname{sign}\sum_{|y|\le d}\hat F(y)\chi_y(x)\) on the 569 tumours. When
most of the weight is at low degree these approximate \(f\) well (Linial, Mansour and Nisan 1993).
\(g_1\) is a weighted vote of the single features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/bernstein_vazirani_spectrum.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

#include <bit>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto cancer = dm::datasets::breast_cancer();
  const std::vector<std::string> features = {"radius", "texture", "perimeter", "area", "smoothness",
                                             "compactness", "concavity", "concave_points", "symmetry",
                                             "fractal_dimension"};
  const int n = static_cast<int>(features.size());
  const std::size_t rows = cancer.nrows(), N = std::size_t{1} << n;

  // Each tumour's corner of the cube: bit i = feature i above its median.
  std::vector<std::size_t> corner(rows, 0);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    const auto values = cancer.get_column("mean_" + features[i]).values();
    auto sorted = values;
    std::ranges::sort(sorted);
    const double median = (sorted[(rows - 1) / 2] + sorted[rows / 2]) / 2.0;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rows; ++r)
      if (values[r] > median) corner[r] |= std::size_t{1} << i;
  }
  std::vector<int> label(rows), vote(N, 0), count(N, 0);
  for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rows; ++r) {
    label[r] = std::get<std::string>(cancer.value("diagnosis", r)) == "malignant";
    vote[corner[r]] += label[r] ? 1 : -1;
    ++count[corner[r]];
  }
  // The oracle: majority diagnosis at each corner, nearest occupied corners when empty.
  std::vector<int> f(N, 0);
  std::size_t occupied = 0;
  for (std::size_t x = 0; x < N; ++x) {
    if (count[x] > 0) {
      ++occupied;
      f[x] = vote[x] > 0;
      continue;
    }
    int best = n + 1, total = 0;
    for (std::size_t z = 0; z < N; ++z) {
      if (count[z] == 0) continue;
      const int d = std::popcount(x ^ z);
      if (d < best) best = d, total = 0;
      if (d == best) total += vote[z];
    }
    f[x] = total > 0;
  }
  std::print("{} tumours occupy {} of the {} corners; the classifier calls {} corners malignant.\n", rows, occupied, N,
             std::ranges::count(f, 1));

  // Quantum circuit against the classical transform.
  const auto psi = alg::fourier_sampling_state(f);
  const auto F = alg::walsh_spectrum(f);
  double gap = 0.0;
  for (std::size_t y = 0; y < N; ++y) gap = std::max(gap, std::abs(psi[y] - dm::algorithms::Complex(F[y], 0.0)));
  std::print("Largest difference between circuit amplitudes and the fast Walsh–Hadamard transform: {:.1e}.\n\n", gap);

  // Spectrum by degree: exact and from 2,000 measurements.
  const std::size_t shots = 2000;
  const auto outcomes = alg::measure_shots(psi, shots, 569);
  std::vector<double> exact(n + 1, 0.0), sampled(n + 1, 0.0);
  for (std::size_t y = 0; y < N; ++y) exact[std::popcount(y)] += F[y] * F[y];
  for (auto y : outcomes) sampled[std::popcount(y)] += 1.0 / shots;
  std::print("| degree \\(k\\) |");
  for (int k = 0; k <= 5; ++k) std::print(" {} |", k);
  std::print(" 6–10 |\n| --- |");
  for (int k = 0; k <= 6; ++k) std::print(" ---: |");
  const auto row = [&](const char* name, const std::vector<double>& w) {
    std::print("\n| {} |", name);
    for (int k = 0; k <= 5; ++k) std::print(" {:.3f} |", w[k]);
    std::print(" {:.3f} |", std::accumulate(w.begin() + 6, w.end(), 0.0));
  };
  row("exact \\(W_k\\)", exact);
  row("2,000 shots", sampled);
  std::print("\n\n");

  // The largest coefficients and how often the circuit returned them.
  std::vector<std::size_t> order(N);
  std::iota(order.begin(), order.end(), 0);
  std::ranges::sort(order, [&](auto a, auto b) { return std::abs(F[a]) > std::abs(F[b]); });
  std::print("| parity \\(y\\) (features) | \\(\\hat F(y)\\) | \\(\\hat F(y)^2\\) | share of shots |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
    const auto y = order[i];
    std::string name;
    for (int b = 0; b < n; ++b)
      if (y >> b & 1) name += (name.empty() ? "" : " ⊕ ") + features[b];
    std::print("| {} | {:+.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", name.empty() ? "(constant)" : name, F[y], F[y] * F[y],
               std::ranges::count(outcomes, y) / static_cast<double>(shots));
  }

  // Low-degree approximations, evaluated on the tumours.
  const auto accuracy = [&](const std::vector<int>& g) {
    std::size_t right = 0;
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rows; ++r) right += g[corner[r]] == label[r];
    return static_cast<double>(right) / rows;
  };
  std::print("\n| classifier | Fourier weight kept | agreement with \\(f\\) on all corners | accuracy on the 569 tumours |\n"
             "| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int d : {0, 1, 2, 3}) {
    std::vector<int> g(N);
    double kept = 0;
    for (int k = 0; k <= d; ++k) kept += exact[k];
    for (std::size_t x = 0; x < N; ++x) {
      double sum = 0;
      for (std::size_t y = 0; y < N; ++y)
        if (std::popcount(y) <= d) sum += F[y] * ((std::popcount(x & y) & 1) ? -1.0 : 1.0);
      g[x] = sum < 0;  // F = (-1)^f, so negative means f = 1
    }
    std::size_t agree = 0;
    for (std::size_t x = 0; x < N; ++x) agree += g[x] == f[x];
    std::print("| \\(g_{}\\) | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", d, kept, agree / static_cast<double>(N), accuracy(g));
  }
  std::print("| \\(f\\) itself | 1.000 | 1.000 | {:.3f} |\n", accuracy(f));

  std::vector<double> k_left, k_right;
  for (int k = 0; k <= n; ++k) {
    k_left.push_back(k - 0.2);
    k_right.push_back(k + 0.2);
  }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(k_left, exact, "exact W_k", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 0.38)
      .bars(k_right, sampled, "2,000 shots", dm::RGB{217, 119, 6}, 0.38)
      .y_limits(0, 0.4)
      .title("Fourier spectrum of the breast-cancer classifier by degree")
      .x_label("degree k (number of features in the parity)")
      .y_label("Fourier weight")
      .size(820, 420)
      .save_svg("build/bernstein_vazirani_spectrum.svg");
}
```
