// result_end

\caption(Fourier weight \(W_k\) of the binarised breast-cancer classifier by degree: exact (blue) and the share of 2,000 simulated Bernstein–Vazirani measurements (orange).)
\alttext(Grouped bar chart of Fourier weight by degree from 0 to 10, exact in blue and from 2,000 shots in orange. The two agree closely: about 0.27 at degree 0, 0.30 at degree 1, 0.11 at degree 2, about 0.1 at degrees 3 and 4, then falling to near zero by degree 8.)

The 569 tumours fall on only 109 of the 1,024 corners, because the ten size and shape features
are strongly correlated. The classifier extends their majority vote to the rest and calls 247
corners malignant. The simulated circuit's amplitudes equal the fast Walsh–Hadamard transform
to \(9\times10^{-16}\), and 2,000 measurements estimate each \(W_k\) within about 0.02, as binomial
noise allows (\(\sqrt{0.3\cdot0.7/2000}=0.01\)).

The constant coefficient \(\hat F(0)=0.518\) records that \(f\) is benign on 75.9% of corners. The
largest non-constant coefficients are all single features: concavity, concave points, texture, area,
radius and perimeter, each with \(\hat F(e_i)\approx0.18\) to \(0.28\). All are positive, meaning an
above-median value goes with "malignant". The first parity of several features is
radius ⊕ perimeter ⊕ area, with a negative sign. Those three features nearly always agree in the
data, and then their parity equals each of them. The negative coefficient partly cancels the
triple counting of one piece of size information by the three degree-1 terms. Degrees 0 and 1
together hold 0.570 of the weight. The low-degree classifiers follow: \(g_1\), a weighted vote of
the single features, agrees with \(f\) on 86.5% of corners and classifies 89.8% of the tumours.
Adding degree 2 and 3 parities brings this to 92.1%, close to the 92.6% of \(f\) itself.

A caveat. The Fourier coefficients weight all 1,024 corners equally, while the data occupy only
109. The spectrum describes the classifier on the whole cube, including the 915 corners it filled
in by nearest neighbours, not the distribution of tumours.

> Summary

- The circuit \(H^{\otimes n}U_fH^{\otimes n}\) leaves the amplitude \(\hat F(y)\), the Walsh–Hadamard
  coefficient of \((-1)^f\), on each \(|y\rangle\). For a parity \(s\cdot x\) this is a single spike at
  \(s\), so one query reads all \(n\) bits, against \(n\) classical queries. The simulator confirms
  probability 1 up to \(n=20\).
- With an oracle wrong on a fraction \(\eta\) of inputs, \(\Pr(s)=(1-2\eta)^2\) exactly. The most
  frequent of repeated outcomes recovers \(s\) until \((1-2\eta)^2\) falls below the largest
  wrong outcome, which happened between \(\eta=0.4\) and \(0.45\) at \(n=10\).
- On a general \(f\) the circuit samples the Fourier spectrum. On a breast-cancer classifier,
  2,000 shots reproduce the degree profile, concavity is the strongest single parity, and the
  degree-1 part alone classifies 89.8% of tumours.
- New library pieces: `algorithms::fourier_sampling_state`, `walsh_spectrum`,
  `parity_truth_table`, `measure_shots` and `bernstein_vazirani_classical`.

